  "Question": "The degree of \\((x^2+1)^4(x^3+1)^3\\) as a polynomial in \\(x\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(5\\)",
    "B": "\\(7\\)",
    "C": "\\(12\\)",
    "D": "\\(17\\)",
    "E": "\\(72\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The degree is the highest exponent with a nonzero coefficient, so there is no need to expand the entire product. In \\((x^2+1)^4\\), choosing \\(x^2\\) from all four copies gives the highest term \\(x^8\\). Similarly, the highest term of \\((x^3+1)^3\\) is \\(x^9\\).\n\nTheir product is\n\\[\nx^8x^9=x^{17}.\n\\]\nIts coefficient is 1. Every other selection uses a constant 1 in place of a positive power of \\(x\\), giving a lower exponent, so the \\(x^{17}\\) term cannot cancel. The degree is \\(\\boxed{17}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
